<tr><td colspan=2>{Exhausted and still bleeding through his armor, Deftnoes Fiir’eal approaches the Wall of Blood.  He makes his way slowly as his arms are burdened with the limp body of a naked half elf.  The creature’s white hair and pale flesh are spattered with drying blood.  Though her wounds are numerous and severe, they cannot match the number of old scars.  <br>
<br>
Kneeling at the foot of the Wall, Deftnoes drops the body to the ground.  The expression on his face twists in pain.  Reaching for his side, he ever so slowly pulls a large dagger free from his armor… not from between chinks in the armor… but from the armor itself.  Fresh blood flows from the wound.  He examines the blade for a moment, sighing as he watches poison drip along the edge of Ragebringer.  <br>
<br>
He presses the pommel of the dagger into her hand before laying her hand atop her chest.  Despite the blood drying on her flesh and the organs visible through gaping wounds, he wonders for a moment if she won’t suddenly lash out now.  It would be the perfect moment for a final strike.  It would be just like her.  But she remains motionless, her violet eyes open and unblinking.  Her father’s eyes.  <br>
<br>
Standing slowly, he turns to face the Horde.}<br>
<br>
“Burn her… feed her to the trolls… doesn’t matter now.  But the body is yours.”<br>
<br>
{He looks down at his hands where he is now fondling a small mask made of Tier’dal flesh.  As one hand closes tight around the mask, his other hand moves to explore the gash Ragebringer has left in his armor.  Turning to disappear into the shadows, his faint mutterings can be heard, “How in the hell am I going to get this repaired?  Damn girl.  Damn girl.”} <br>
<br>
OOC:<br>
Well, it’s been a good run for me.  From the early days on Karana when we thought bronze armor was uber to seeing the Horde reach the planes and obtain epics for their members.  Leadership and membership have changed and changed.  The Horde has certainly had its ups and downs.  But we’ve always proven to be a guild that exists beyond the sum of its parts.  Honestly, I adored some of you, admired some of you, was annoyed by some of you, and down right wanted to strangle some of you.  But, I always wanted to do right by all of you.  Because you are Horde.  And if this guild knows one thing, it’s the meaning of family.  From the angry bickering to the undying loyalty.  <br>
<br>
I did not want to leave the game or any of you, but given the current circumstances… I can see no other way.  As most of you know, Gnorm is my rl partner in sin (i.e. we’re unmarried and living together and making my mother a very unhappy woman).  And after this last hack, he just can’t stomach staying.  I hope all of you understand that even given these facts, to leave… is so very hard.  <br>
<br>
So, I know you’re all going to miss the way I’d flip out if combat was sloppy… or the way that Deftnoes, Gnormagn and I were so casually cruel with each other as a means of camaraderie.  Okay, maybe you won’t miss that stuff.  But I KNOW Rekka is going to miss the butt pants.  To those who never got to know me, I’m going to reiterate the advice that being pro-active and being known for being good at what you do always pays off.  To those I pissed off at any point, either you deserved it or you misunderstood.  *smirk*  How’s that for a pompous statement?  I took lessons from Deft.  <br>
<br>
And to all those I adored… to all the Horde… thank you for making this game fun.  Thank you for being the reason to log on.  The reason to level.  The reason to want to be better and do better.  Thanks for putting up with my posted stories and demented role-playing.  I am still moved when I think back on all the generosity, warmth, support, and encouragement I have received from so many of you.   <br>
<br>
I’m giving DAoC a try, and though I am already finding many things I like about it… I can already tell that it will never be what EQ was.  The Horde, as always, has spoiled me.  For those who wish to keep in touch my email is always open. (furryblueweirdo@home.com)     <br>
<br>
And now, the thank yous and remembrances start rolling out.  <br>
<br>
Thank you Zhokaii, for starting this guild, for being a friend, and for never ceasing to be fun.  Kolu and Runja, thank you for making me feel welcome when I came in the form of a half elf and for being such inspirational members worth looking up to.  Thank you Voness, for helping me with some rogueish questions, and equipment, and CR.  Thank you for being such a talented rogue that I had to admire your skill, even when I wanted to strangle you for being such an amazing prick.  Kloth, Razze, Izayo, Jury, Ithgor… being good enough to hunt with all of you had long been my goal.  Not high level enough, but good enough at my job.  You inspired me, with your skill and with your willingness to help out the Horde.  I speak of all of you as if you were still a part of this family, because in my mind… you always will be.     <br>
<br>
Inertia, Achzizra, Rekka, the three of you held this guild together one of the many times it threatened to fall apart, and the three of you have brought this guild farther than it has ever gone before.  Rekka, I hated you so much at first.  Even as I respected what you had done for the Horde, I wanted to scream at your attitude.  But somewhere in there we learned to work together… till I had to down right adore you for being such a great guy.  I still feel the urge to smack you at times, but always with a grin.  Your loyalty and talent cannot be denied.  Thank you for doing what you do.<br>
<br>
Achzizra, when I returned the last time, it was so good to see that the Horde had found a member like you.  Proactive, social, and always willing to help.  I remember, when Gnorm and I came back… your Black Dire fight was one of the first things we did.  Even so early there was something about your manner that made me glow to help you out.  Something selfless but determined in the way you went about things.  Your spirit is the spirit that has held the Horde together and defined us as a family.  <br>
<br>
Inertia, my Warmistress… I remember watching your name fly by in guild chat when I was just a little thing.  I remember wondering when I’d be good enough to hunt with you.  And I remember absolutely beaming on those days when you actually came to ToFS to see what it was that kept me, Gnorm and Deft exploring that place.  You showed me what a mage could do.  And you fought with us as someone fighting with us, not as a babysitter.  I saw then, before you rose to your current level, how serious you were about fairness and generosity.  With you leading the Horde, I know that things will be done according to merit.  I know that you will always break your back for the Horde, and you know such commitment when you see it in others.  Thank you for being there with me as I ended Kartane’s life in Norrath.  Thank you for understanding why I have to go.  Just thank you for being yourself.<br>
<br>
Thank you Dakuel, Shaar, Wascen, Deftnoes, Jezelle, Kolu, Razze, Zhokaii, Voness, Izayo, Inertia, Achzizra, Freyja, Gneil, Gnormagn, Skrabian, Pustule, Boilybut, Ulul, Vengerax, Kloth, Arazul and many others for presenting me with fun and interesting role play.  Sure, it’s harder to do at higher levels, but nothing cuts through down time like a nice IC argument.  Tracking the character development of Kartane was a bumpy road.  But Deftnoes gets special kudos for being the only one besides Gnorm to watch Kartane go all the way from a bastard child reaching for acceptance to a religiously crazed woman who lived only for the Horde and for the kill.  Thanks to Jezelle for being the big sister and I never wanted and to Kitsune for being the little sister I never had.        <br>
<br>
Thank you to all the Hordlings, simply for being a part of this family.  Never forget to do us proud.  Never forget what you are representing out there in the world of Norrath.  Show style.  Show class.  And show them that you can role-play your little bum off even as you work with your group as a shining example of your class.<br>
<br>
Thank you all of Scarlet Underground, for being great allies.  Of course, special thanks goes to Kitzu, Bearwolf, Sirik, Dopple and Vlaq for being so terribly skilled.  Kitzu pulls with a precision to rival Kloth’s and kicks ass with a passion to rival Kartane’s.  Bearwolf is always so useful, nice and funny… that I constantly forget she is a druid.  After Gnormagn, of course, Sirik is the cleric I trust most with my life.  She has shown me not only that she’s skilled, but that she’s a thoughtful, sincere person.  Dopple… you rock.  You’re on the ball.  You know how to work the room.  You’re so damn skilled.  And well… YOU’RE A GNOME!  And Vlaq… how can I ever thank you for helping me to make Kartane’s death some good in the world of Norrath.  Your words went to heart and I thank you for being a friend to me in those final moments.  I don’t think either guild should ever run our of essence emeralds now. <br>
<br>
Thanks to Shadow Council.  Razze, Glicknib, Canddie, Dogge… you boys get special kisses.  For thinking of me and helping me out on my epic.  For always being fun and reliable to group with.  For smack talking, leg humping, fair looting, skilled fighting, bitch slapping madness.<br>
<br>
Another round of thanks to Pustule, Skrabian, Deftnoes, Rekka, Norb, Annilaotor, Aaelaelinae, Freyja, Dopple, Bearwolf, Glicknib, Canddie, Kloth, Inertia, and Kitzu for raising your hands whenever Gnorm asked, “Who wants to go hunt (insert name of new place we’re exploring this week) with us?”  Thanks for sharing some of my passion for a good dungeon crawl where tactics are key.  What good is leveling if you don’t know what to do with it?  And Freyja, special thanks for the time you put in at the ToFS.  It’s not an easy place to deal with, but you kicked ass with the best of us in there… and you learned the lesson of the Tower with us.  Some days you own the Tower.  And some days the Tower owns you.<br>
<br>
<br>
I suppose I’ve saved the best for last… at least the longest.  Deftnoes… for being such a great SK that you allowed me to be a fantastic rogue.  You were so good and so constant that I was allowed to be the rogue I wanted to be.  Screw worrying about my AC.  I could be the true finesse fighter that I dreamed a rogue to be.  I had no worries.  I wasn’t going to get hit.  Every mob was lined up and held just right so that I never missed a chance at a backstab.  We reached a precision that was second nature by the end.  The down to the wire healing from Gnorm.  The relentless taunting from you.  And the solid damage from me.  Efficient.  Precise.  Deadly.  Such an amazing team we were.  <br>
<br>
Rather odd to think back on those days in Cazic Thule, when I barely knew you and thought you were a pompous ass full of bravado.  I respected you all the more when I saw you had a right to that bravado.  Deftnoes was Kartane’s sword brother.  And you were, and still are, my friend.  Working together we made each other better players, to the point that now… we find ourselves a little lost without the others.  I find myself so often spoiled by competence.  And my solution to this has angered many.  I do not compromise.  Do it right or get lost.  But who better to teach me that sometimes, you just have to be as ass if you want to do things right… than you?<br>
<br>
I know that leaving the Horde hurt you, as much as this hurts me.  But when the heart is gone from the game… the choices are painful.  I had to kill Kartane.  Because what is she without her gnome and SK?  What is she when I cannot love the game anymore?  And I’d rather Kartane be dead than to wander Norrath as a soulless creature.  It’s the heart and soul of this game, this guild, its members that has always kept me in the game.  And for me, exploring and breaking new zones, inventing new tactics, maximizing skills were all a part of this.  It has always been the experiences, not the xp, that mattered.<br>
<br>
I got to do a lot of the things I wanted to.  We did break the ToFS.  We did explore Siren’s Grotto.  I’ve heard the ghosts of a dragon screaming in Dragon Necropolis.  I’ve tapped into plotlines and tried to piece together mysteries.  I not only got my epic, but did so with the help and encouragement of three guilds.  I’ve pulled corpses from impossible places.  Okay, I didn’t pull off that time I tried to drag past Vox… and boy was she pissed.  I’ve seen and fought on four planes.  And I never stopped having fun with any of it.  And for that… I thank you… all of you…                <br>
<br>
I always tried to do you proud… and kill everything in sight,<br>
Dana          <br>
</td></tr><tr><td colspan=2>&nbsp;</td></tr>

